# HANDOFF TO MASTER — Q_k THEORY MILESTONE 1

## 1. Strongest new result

For a fixed nontrivial variable bipartition of sizes `p,q`, let `R_r^{p,q}` be Boolean functions whose truth matrix has GF(2)-rank at most `r`. Then

- `kappa(R_1^{p,q}) = max(p,q)`;
- `kappa(R_r^{p,q}) = p+q` for every `r>=2`.

Thus the worst-case bounded-conditioned-count reconstruction threshold jumps sharply between rank 1 and rank 2. Static low GF(2) rank does not imply persistence under low-order conditioned-count queries.

## 2. Exact theorem statement

Let `Q_k` contain every indexed conditioned count obtained by fixing at most `k` coordinates. For `p,q>=1`, `n=p+q`, and the fixed `p|q` flattening:

`Q_max(p,q)` is injective on rank-<=1 truth matrices, and no smaller `Q_k` is injective. By contrast, rank-<=2 already contains the global even/odd parity pair, which is indistinguishable under `Q_(n-1)`, so rank-<=r has threshold `n` for every `r>=2`.

